fontselect: Android's list of its fonts where it has no font matcher

Android's provider found a letter's font by asking AFontMatcher, which came
with Android 10 (API 29). Built for Android 9, libass could not call it at
all, and a library built for 10 does not load on 9.

On Android 9 the provider now goes by the list the system's own text stack
is set up from, /system/etc/fonts.xml (libass/ass_android_fonts.c, which
has nothing of libass or Android in it and is checked on the Mac by
Scripts/check-native.sh): the default family, then the families the system
falls back on, in the list's order, the first whose font has the letter.
Of a script's two families the one made for running text is taken, as the
matcher is asked for it, and a family's bold is listed with it where it is
a file of its own. That is the text stack's order less its weighing of the
reader's languages, which decides between the Chinese, Japanese and Korean
forms of a Han character and nothing else; Han characters are the default
font's, which the application chose for the reader, and never reach the
provider.

A font found is kept open, as the matcher's are; one that lacks the letter
is closed again, since a face is the whole file mapped and a list has some
two hundred families. A letter no font has is looked for in every file
once, and remembered.

On Android 10 and later nothing changes: AFontMatcher is asked as before.
Its functions are weak references now (__ANDROID_UNAVAILABLE_SYMBOLS_ARE_WEAK__),
null on Android 9, and every call of them stands behind a check of the
system's version, which the compiler holds the file to
(-Werror=unguarded-availability in the build script).

